    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/8/88972.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 75,
    "turn_text": "First of all, let me applaud you. That might be the most awesome question I've ever been, I've ever been asked. How about, how about, the season is wrong, but sometimes it snows in April. It's pretty spare, it's pretty simple arrangement, but it talks about both despair, but also hope, right? And so I imagine there are some Dems, both in the Commonwealth and around the country who sort of Certainly late Tuesday night, maybe early Wednesday, felt sense of despair. But I would tell them all Wednesday, the sun rose, the oceans didn't recede, no meteor struck the earth. So out of there the hope comes despair. It's actually one of my favorite print songs, just because it's so simple and it's so poignant to me. In some sense, that's kind of what election is about. It's such a simple, but poignant civic process. Simply get up on that election day, go cast a ballot for the person or people you think are best prepared and able to tackle the issues that we care most about or at least we ought to care most about. I wish more people with both vote and go listen to sometimes the snows in April.",
