  {
    "episode_id": 88972,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/8/88972.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 77,
    "turn_text": "You know, I think it's actually in the Republican leadership's interest to actually try to find common ground with the president. And look, as soon as the 2014 midterms are over a week ago, the conversation really shifted to 2016. The Republicans want to win back the White House. if they want to have a chance of doing so i think they need to show that now that they're in charge of congress congress uh... will seem to be something uh... will seem to be needs to be less dysfunctional and they need to put some wins on the board and to do that because they don't have a super majority they're going to need to work with the democrats not just the democrats in congress but the white house so at the same time uh... candidly respectfully i think the president uh... as he's nearing the end of his term needs to put some points on the board, too. And he's put a lot up there. And frankly, I think, with respect to my colleagues in the Senate, I think they might have done more in running with this president and running on his record, which in some very important categories is very strong, economically, however you feel about the health care law, the fact that more and more people have insurance. I mean, there's a long line, the economy's growing at 3.2 percent, you know, and that all-important metric How much are you paying for gas? Gas is less than $3 a gallon. That's all happening in an Obama administration.",
