    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/8/88972.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 82,
    "turn_text": "So it turns out, and I'm breaking this news for the first time. So Leader McConnell and I both wrestled in high school. So every Thursday at 11 a.m., we'd gather in his palatial office and we'd actually wrestle. I'm joking. I'm joking, of course. I wish I could tell you that I spent a lot of time with Mitch McConnell. And however you may feel about him, here's one thing I will say about him. I actually do think he's a serious man. serious thinker, and obviously fiercely protective of his party and principles. The morning I was, the governor announced he was appointing me to the Senate. As his tradition, you reach out to the Senate leaders, both Harry Reid and Mitch McConnell, and you were told the protocol. You don't refer to either one as Senator, you refer to them both as leaders, ones the majority leader, ones the minority leader. I call leader reads office we have a very short but very pleasant conversation most conversations with Harry reader pretty short and then we had arranged the time for me to call leader McConnell's office and I get on the phone and his assistant says hold and he comes on the phone he's got this really deep rich Kentucky draw and he says well mr. Cowan is it welcome to the Klan and I said Thanks. But he was a very, by the way, that's what the sea for those out there, keeping score. But he was a very, in my time in the Senate while our interactions were few and far between. He was always very cordial with me. We spoke a little bit after the marathon bombing because one of the victims actually had roots, had some ties to Kentucky. We talked about that a little bit. You know, and believe it or not, and this is hard for people to believe, there's a far more collegiality in the chamber than is ever portrayed publicly. And like most members of the Senate, Leader McConnell, you know, warmly welcomed me into the body and we had a very cordial relationship. Again, did not spend much time together. One of the more interesting things I noticed every morning The tradition is the two leaders come to the floor to open the Senate, and they often talk about each other, but rarely talk to each other. And so it's fascinating to see that, particularly when I was sitting in the presiding officer's chair, because when someone is talking on the Senate floor, the person they're actually speaking to is the presiding officer. And so Republican or Democrat, you listen to everybody's viewpoint on every issue. And so you could see the interpersonal relationships or lack thereof among some of the members.",
